/pack/*
/packuse/*
/blink/*
/bak/*
/*.usr.opt
/**/pfw.dev.*
*.dmp
*.log
*.bak
*.bat
*.zip
*.rar
thumbs.db
!/res/*.zip
!/samples/*.zip

# ================================================================================================
#  THE .NET BUILD OUTPUT OF THE MIGRATION TREE.
#
#  Everything above this banner is the legacy PowerBuilder repository's own ignore set and is left
#  exactly as it stands: no pattern is edited, reordered or removed, and the two negations that
#  re-admit the packaged archives under res/ and samples/ still apply to the patterns above them.
#  This block is purely additive.
#
#  WHY IT IS HERE AT ALL, WHICH IS A CORRECTION. The migration plan records that no .gitignore
#  change is required. That was true of the legacy tree and is not true of the tree beside it: a
#  clean clone followed by the documented per-service command - dotnet restore, dotnet build -c
#  Release, dotnet test - writes bin/ and obj/ under twenty projects and leaves the working tree
#  DIRTY, which contradicts the requirement that each service build and test independently from a
#  clean checkout (C-I) and makes `git status` useless as the review instrument it has to be. The
#  patterns lived only in .git/info/exclude, which is per-clone and is not committed - so the
#  property held for whoever created that clone and for nobody else.
#
#  UNANCHORED AND TRAILING-SLASHED, DELIBERATELY. bin/ and obj/ appear at every project root, so an
#  anchored pattern would have to be repeated twenty times and would be wrong on the twenty-first.
#  The trailing slash restricts each to a DIRECTORY, so a source file that happens to be named
#  bin or obj is unaffected.
#
#  NOTHING HERE SUPPRESSES A SOURCE PATH. Neither name is a directory this refactor authors, and the
#  CI target-scope audit enumerates every tracked file outside the legacy tree, so a source file
#  hidden by one of these patterns would surface there as a missing member of its declared group.
# ================================================================================================

bin/
obj/

# The test host's own result directory. Written by `dotnet test` alongside the coverage report the
# 80-percent gate is measured from, and never an input to anything.
TestResults/

# The end-to-end suite's installed closure and run artifacts. The suite's own nested ignore file
# already covers these for anyone working inside tests/e2e; these entries make the same guarantee
# hold for a `git status` run from the repository root, which is where the review instrument runs.
node_modules/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
test-results/

# ================================================================================================
#  THE POPULATED ENVIRONMENT FILE. THIS IS A SECRETS CONTROL (C-F), NOT HOUSEKEEPING.
#
#  WHAT IT PREVENTS, CONCRETELY. The documented bring-up is `cp .env.example .env` followed by
#  filling that copy in, and what gets filled in is the twelve host PATHS plus - when the operator
#  adopts the optional issuance identity - `SECURITY_CLIENT_SECRET`, whose value IS a credential.
#  Without a pattern here the populated copy is an UNTRACKED file that `git status` lists, that
#  `git add -A` stages without comment, and that a commit then publishes. Every other control this
#  repository has against committed key material - the CI target-scope audit, the no-credential-in-
#  settings assertions, the options-pattern injection - operates on files the tree already tracks, so
#  none of them sees the one file the bring-up instructions tell an operator to create.
#
#  THE MITIGATIONS THAT WERE ALREADY IN PLACE WERE PROSE, AND PROSE IS NOT A CONTROL.
#  orchestration/README.md and .env.example both warn against committing the file and both suggest
#  adding it to .git/info/exclude. That advice stands and is worth keeping as belt-and-braces, but
#  .git/info/exclude is PER-CLONE and is not committed - so the protection held for whoever had read
#  the warning and applied it, and for nobody else. This entry is the committed form of it, and it is
#  the same correction the .NET build-output block above records for the same reason.
#
#  ANCHORED AND EXACT, WHICH IS THE DELIBERATE CHOICE. `/orchestration/.env` names the one path the
#  documented bring-up creates. A bare `.env` or `**/.env` would additionally hide any future .env
#  anywhere in the tree, including one a suite legitimately tracks as a FIXTURE - and a pattern that
#  hides files nobody meant to hide is how a source path goes missing without a diagnostic. The
#  TEMPLATE is deliberately NOT matched: `orchestration/.env.example` is tracked, is the authoritative
#  variable inventory, and carries no value for any secret.
#
#  THE PER-SERVICE OVERRIDE FORMS ARE MATCHED TOO, because an operator running two stacks on one host
#  names its file something like `.env.local` and the same exposure applies to it verbatim. The
#  negation that follows re-admits the template, and it must stay AFTER the wildcard: git applies the
#  last matching pattern, so reversing the two lines would re-hide the template.
# ================================================================================================

/orchestration/.env
/orchestration/.env.*
!/orchestration/.env.example
